Switch Statement with Characters in C

Today, I practiced using the switch statement with character values in the C programming language.

Through this program, I learned:

How to declare a character variable using the char data type.
How to initialize a character variable with '\0'.
How to use the switch statement to check different character values.
How to use single quotes (' ') for character constants.
How case statements match the value of a variable.
How the break statement prevents execution from continuing into the next case.
How the default case handles invalid input.
How to display different messages using printf().
Important Logic / Formula

Switch Case Logic

char day – Declares a character variable.
switch(day) – Checks the value of the day variable.
case – Matches the given character value.
break – Exits the switch statement.
default – Displays an error message for invalid input.

Important Note:

In C, a char variable can store only one character, not a string such as "Mon" or "Tue". 
Also, case 'Mon' is not a valid way to compare a string.

To make this program work with inputs like Mon, Tue, and Wed, we need to use a string (char[]) and compare it using strcmp().

Project Goal

To understand how the switch statement works with character values in C. 
This program helped me practice character variables, case labels, break statements, default cases, and decision-making logic. 
I also learned the difference between characters and strings in C.